 q[1]['options'][0]['explanation']='NP-completeness concerns the satisfiability decision problem. Given an assignment, checking its clauses requires no search.'
 q[1]['workedExample']=q[1]['workedExample'].replace('here a few million lookups: milliseconds.', 'plus clause bookkeeping; the prompt does not specify clause lengths or a measured runtime.')
 q[2]['options'][0]['explanation']='A clause is an OR of its literals; clauses are combined by AND. The zero token terminates a clause, regardless of line breaks.'
 q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace('Literals on a line are ORed.', 'Literals up to the next zero terminator are ORed; a clause may span lines.')
 q[6]['workedExample']=q[6]['workedExample'].replace('A useful rule: adding options to a problem (longer clauses) can only make it harder, so k-SAT for k ≥ 3 is NP-complete.', 'For fixed k ≥ 3, allowing clauses of at most k literals includes every 3-SAT instance unchanged, proving NP-hardness; polynomial certificate checking gives membership in NP. This inclusion argument is not a general rule about adding options to arbitrary problems.')
 q[9]['workedExample']=q[9]['workedExample'].replace('it stops the solver ever exploring x1 = T, x4 = F again anywhere in the search.', 'while retained, it prevents propagation from accepting that conflicting combination. Learned-clause deletion can remove this protection.')
 q[12]['options'][1]['explanation']='The standard guarantee is equisatisfiability and projection of models, not logical equivalence over all assignments to original and fresh variables.'
 q[11]['workedExample']+='\nHere the clauses already force all four variable values, so minimizing installed packages changes nothing in this example.'
